/T (The directory hierarchy starts at /. Common paths: /etc configuration, /var/log logs, /home user files, /usr programs and libraries, /opt optional software, /tmp temporary files and /dev device nodes. The Virtual File System provides a common interface to filesystems such as ext4 and XFS. Permissions define read/write/execute for owner, group and others. Setuid runs a program with its file owner's effective identity and must be audited. sudo grants approved administrative commands. Run the portal as a dedicated non-root account.)
/E (The directory hierarchy starts at /. Common paths: /etc configuration, /var/log logs, /home user files, /usr programs and libraries, /opt optional software, /tmp temporary files and /dev device nodes. The Virtual File System provides a common interface to filesystems such as ext4 and XFS. Permissions define read/write/execute for owner, group and others. Setuid runs a program with its file owner's effective identity and must be audited. sudo grants approved administrative commands. Run the portal as a dedicated non-root account.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
